                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/8848: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bullitt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-07-19T21:00:00+01:00">July 19, 1937—9
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 19—6 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1008. Delbos in the course of our conversation this evening expressed to me
                    extreme pessimism with regard to the situation in the Far East. He said that he
                    did not wish to bring forward any proposals for the settlement of the dispute;
                    but it had occurred to him that inasmuch as all the great powers had a right to
                    have the Peiping-Tientsin Railroad kept open that right might be a good basis on
                    which the great powers might make a simultaneous <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >démarche</hi> in both Tokyo and Nanking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
